      "source": [
        "# 00 - Text Incident EDA\\n",
        "\\n",
        "## Problem framing\\n",
        "\\n",
        "SafetyOps Copilot ingests free-text incident descriptions (e.g., near-misses, injuries, unsafe conditions) and must:\\n",
        "\\n",
        "- Assign a **category** (fall, electrical, fire, chemical, other).\\n",
        "- Estimate **severity** (low, medium, high).\\n",
        "- Provide a confidence score and optional explanations later.\\n",
        "\\n",
        "In v1, we implement a **rule-based classifier** as a baseline. This notebook documents how we would approach EDA and modeling once a real dataset is available."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Dataset plan\\n",
        "\\n",
        "We do **not** commit real incident data into the repo. Instead, we plan to:\\n",
        "\\n",
        "1. Define a data contract for text incidents (fields like `text`, `timestamp`, `site`, `reported_by`, `labels`).\\n",
        "2. Provide helper scripts (or documentation) to:\\n",
        "   - Download or export data from internal systems (e.g., safety ticketing tools).\\n",
        "   - Anonymize / pseudonymize sensitive fields.\\n",
        "   - Store data locally for EDA (e.g., under a non-committed `data/raw/` folder).\\n",
        "3. Use small synthetic subsets for examples in notebooks only.\\n",
        "\\n",
        "Expected input format (conceptual):\\n",
        "\\n",
        "```text\\n",
        "incident_id, timestamp, site, reporter_role, text, label_category, label_severity\\n",
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## EDA checklist\\n",
        "\\n",
        "When we have data, we should explore:\\n",
        "\\n",
        "- **Label distribution**\\n",
        "  - Counts of categories (`fall`, `electrical`, `fire`, `chemical`, `other`).\\n",
        "  - Counts of severity levels (`low`, `medium`, `high`).\\n",
        "- **Class imbalance**\\n",
        "  - Rare categories / severities that may need re-weighting or resampling.\\n",
        "- **Text length and structure**\\n",
        "  - Tokens per incident, vocabulary size.\\n",
        "  - Presence of structured patterns (e.g., forms vs free-form notes).\\n",
        "- **Site / region effects**\\n",
        "  - Differences in language across sites, regions, or reporter roles.\\n",
        "- **Temporal patterns**\\n",
        "  - Incident frequencies over time (day/week/month).\\n",
        "  - Relationship between time of day and severity.\\n",
        "- **Data quality**\\n",
        "  - Missing values, duplicate incidents, inconsistent labels."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Baseline model idea\\n",
        "\\n",
        "Initial baselines should be **simple but strong enough** to highlight value:\\n",
        "\\n",
        "1. **Rule-based baseline** (already implemented for v1)\\n",
        "   - Keyword patterns for categories and severity.\\n",
        "   - Deterministic, explainable, easy to maintain.\\n",
        "\\n",
        "2. **Classical ML baseline**\\n",
        "   - Represent text with TF-IDF n-grams.\\n",
        "   - Train a linear model (e.g., Logistic Regression) for:\\n",
        "     - Category prediction.\\n",
        "     - Severity prediction.\\n",
        "   - This can be implemented with scikit-learn.\\n",
        "\\n",
        "3. **Transformer-based baseline (later)**\\n",
        "   - Fine-tune a small transformer (e.g., DistilBERT) on incident text.\\n",
        "   - Use the model to jointly predict category and severity or two separate heads.\\n",
        "   - Track experiments in MLflow."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Metrics rationale\\n",
        "\\n",
        "For both category and severity prediction, we care about:\\n",
        "\\n",
        "- **Precision / Recall / F1** (macro and weighted)\\n",
        "  - Macro-F1: treats all classes equally (good for imbalance awareness).\\n",
        "  - Weighted-F1: accounts for class frequency.\\n",
        "- **Confusion matrix**\\n",
        "  - Highlights systematic confusions between categories (e.g., `fall` vs `other`).\\n",
        "  - Helps prioritize data collection and rule tweaks.\\n",
        "- **Calibration**\\n",
        "  - Are predicted probabilities aligned with empirical frequencies?\\n",
        "  - Poor calibration can mislead operators, especially for high-severity incidents.\\n",
        "\\n",
        "Later, we might also track:\\n",
        "\\n",
        "- **Cost-weighted metrics**\\n",
        "  - Penalize misclassifications that underestimate severity more heavily.\\n",
        "- **Per-site performance**\\n",
        "  - Identify sites or regions where the model underperforms."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Explainability plan\\n",
        "\\n",
        "Even for non-LLM models, explainability is important for trust and audits.\\n",
        "\\n",
        "Potential tools and approaches:\\n",
        "\\n",
        "- **Global insights**\\n",
        "  - Most informative n-grams per class for linear models.\\n",
        "  - Aggregate feature importance from tree-based baselines.\\n",
        "- **Local explanations**\\n",
        "  - LIME (Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations) for specific incidents.\\n",
        "  - Integrated Gradients or similar methods for transformer models.\\n",
        "\\n",
        "In the UI, we can surface a simple version of this (e.g., highlighted keywords) without exposing complex internals."
      ]
    },
